1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,997
Insulin resistance affects more than 2 in 5 US adults, and most don't even know it.

2
00:00:05,317 --> 00:00:10,838
It happens when your cells stop responding well to insulin, so blood sugar creeps up over time.

3
00:00:11,158 --> 00:00:12,600
Three habits make it worse.

4
00:00:12,920 --> 00:00:14,406
First, visceral fat.

5
00:00:14,726 --> 00:00:23,378
Fat packed deep around your organs releases fatty acids and inflammatory chemicals that block insulin from moving glucose into your cells.

6
00:00:23,698 --> 00:00:25,024
Second, added sugar.

7
00:00:25,344 --> 00:00:35,351
A 7-week trial found that sugary drinks trigger fat production in the liver even in moderate doses, and that liver fat buildup drives insulin resistance right where it starts.

8
00:00:35,671 --> 00:00:36,953
Third, chronic stress.

9
00:00:37,273 --> 00:00:46,027
Elevated cortisol promotes visceral fat storage, and more visceral fat leads to more cortisol, creating a cycle that keeps blunting insulin sensitivity.

10
00:00:46,347 --> 00:00:51,358
Losing just 5 to 7 percent of body weight can cut diabetes risk in half.

11
00:00:51,678 --> 00:01:02,297
Swapping sugary drinks for water or whole fruit eases the load on your liver, and short stress breaks like a walk or a few minutes of breathing exercises help break the cortisol cycle.
